`ValueOrDefault` is templated on the default literal type. Using `0/100/50` makes `T=int`, so any JS number that is not an integer may get truncated during `ConvertFromV8`. Since these Cocoa properties are `double` (`minValue/maxValue/doubleValue`), prefer double defaults to preserve precision and avoid unintended narrowing.

```suggestion
  item.slider.minValue = settings.ValueOrDefault("minValue", 0.0);
  item.slider.maxValue = settings.ValueOrDefault("maxValue", 100.0);
  item.slider.doubleValue = settings.ValueOrDefault("value", 50.0);
```

`ValueOrDefault` can be surprising for some `T`:
- Requires `T` to be default-constructible (`T{}`) even if the key is missing.
- `default_value` is taken by value, so it always copies/moves even when the key exists.
- `Get()` expects an output pointer; if callers use pointer types as `T` (e.g. `int*`), `T{}` yields `nullptr` and you end up passing `int**` to `Get`, likely not what’s intended.
Consider constraining `T` (or documenting expectations) and changing the signature to avoid unnecessary copies, e.g. `const T& default_value` / perfect-forwarding, and use `std::optional<T>` internally or `T value; if (Get(...)) return value;` when `T` is default-constructible.

```suggestion
  template <typename T>
    T ValueOrDefault(std::string_view key, const T& default_value) const {
      static_assert(std::is_default_constructible_v<T>, "T must be default-constructible");
      T value{};
      if (Get(key, &value))
        return value;
      return default_value;
    }
```

